\description{
To specify a directed planted partition model, you must specify
the number of nodes (via \code{n}), the number of blocks (via \code{k}), the
edge probabilities (either via \code{within_block} and \code{between_block}, or
via \code{a} and \code{b}), and the block sizes (optional, via \code{pi} or
\code{block_sizes}). By default, the communities a node sends edges from and
receives edges into are sampled independently; set \code{shared_blocks = TRUE}
to give each node a single community. We \strong{strongly recommend}
setting the \code{expected_out_degree}, \code{expected_in_degree}, or
\code{expected_density} argument to avoid large memory allocations
associated with sampling large, dense graphs.
}
\details{
A directed planted partition model is a directed stochastic blockmodel
with \code{k} outgoing blocks and \code{k} incoming blocks, in which the diagonal
and the off-diagonal of the mixing matrix \code{B} are both constant. This
means that the probability of an edge from node \eqn{i} to node
\eqn{j} depends only on whether the outgoing block of \eqn{i} matches
the incoming block of \eqn{j}, but the particular blocks themselves
don't have any impact apart from this. It is a special case of
\code{\link[=directed_dcsbm]{directed_dcsbm()}} where all degree-heterogeneity parameters are equal
to one.
}
\section{Generative Model}{
\subsection{Block memberships}{

Each node \eqn{i} has an outgoing block membership \eqn{z_{out, i}},
which governs the edges it sends, and an incoming block membership
\eqn{z_{in, i}}, which governs the edges it receives. Both take values
in \verb{1, ..., k}. By default, the outgoing memberships are a uniformly
random assignment of nodes to blocks with block sizes that are as
balanced as possible (as in \code{\link[=planted_partition]{planted_partition()}}), or with exactly
\code{block_sizes[j]} nodes in block \code{j} when \code{block_sizes} is specified. The
incoming memberships are assigned in the same way, independently of the
outgoing memberships. When \code{pi} is specified, memberships are instead
sampled independently across nodes from a categorical distribution with
parameter \eqn{\pi} (again, independently for outgoing and incoming
blocks).

When \code{shared_blocks = TRUE}, a single membership vector is sampled
and \eqn{z_{out, i} = z_{in, i}} for every node.
}

\subsection{Mixing matrix}{

The mixing matrix is the \code{k} by \code{k} matrix with \code{within_block} (or
\code{a / n}) on the diagonal and \code{between_block} (or \code{b / n}) everywhere
else, i.e.

\deqn{
  B = p_{between} \mathbf{1} \mathbf{1}^T + (p_{within} - p_{between}) I_k,
}{
  B = p_between * 1 1^T + (p_within - p_between) * I_k,
}

where \eqn{p_{within}} is \code{within_block} and \eqn{p_{between}} is
\code{between_block}. If you specify
\code{expected_out_degree}, \code{expected_in_degree} or \code{expected_density}, \code{B}
is rescaled by a constant, so only the ratio of \code{within_block} to
\code{between_block} matters.
}

\subsection{Edge formation}{

Given the block memberships, each directed edge \eqn{A_{ij}} from node
\eqn{i} to node \eqn{j} is sampled independently (for every ordered
pair \eqn{(i, j)}, so the adjacency matrix is not symmetric) with
expectation

\deqn{
  E[A_{ij}] = B_{z_{out, i}, z_{in, j}}.
}{
  E[A[i, j]] = B[z_out[i], z_in[j]].
}

Edges are Poisson distributed when \code{poisson_edges = TRUE} (yielding a
multigraph) and Bernoulli distributed with the same probabilities when
\code{poisson_edges = FALSE}. Self-loops are allowed or not according to
\code{allow_self_loops}. Note that with the default \code{shared_blocks = FALSE}
and \code{sort_nodes = TRUE}, rows and columns of \eqn{A} are sorted
independently, so the diagonal entry \eqn{A_{ii}} pairs the \eqn{i}-th
sender with the \eqn{i}-th receiver rather than node \eqn{i} with
itself (see \code{sort_nodes}).
}

\subsection{Interpretation}{

When \code{within_block > between_block}, nodes in outgoing block \eqn{s}
preferentially send edges to nodes in incoming block \eqn{s}. With the
default independent memberships, this is a co-clustering structure:
the communities that describe who a node sends edges to are unrelated
to the communities that describe who sends edges to it. With
\code{shared_blocks = TRUE}, each node has one community, and edges in both
directions are more common within communities than between them.
}
}

